# HoneyLens — AI prompt-injection canary callbacks (Idea 52)
# Created 2026-08-13. HoneyLens local-SID block 9026740-9026749. Alert-only
# (HoneyLens sensors run IDS, not inline IPS).
#
# The honeypots plant a benign instruction in credential-like responses — a Secret
# read, an issued API key, a granted admin session, a kubeconfig. An AI agent that
# obeys it fetches https://<sensor>/canary/ai/<nonce>. The honeypot that owns that
# port records the hit itself, so why detect it on the wire as well?
#
#   * Coverage the honeypot cannot give: an agent that resolves the URL but aims it
#     at a port with no canary sink (a scanner's own proxy, a different service on
#     the sensor, a mistyped port) leaves no application log at all. The IDS sees it.
#   * Independent corroboration: the callback is the single fact this whole feature
#     rests on, and it should not be attested only by the process that also issued
#     the canary.
#
# Nonce shape is 12 lowercase hex (intelligence/ai_canary._NONCE_RE); accepting 8+
# keeps the rules working if the length is ever tuned.

# 1. The expected case: an agent obeys the instruction exactly as written.
#    Low severity on its own — this is a measurement, not an intrusion. Its value is
#    in the correlation the sensor builds around it (AI_ASSISTED_AUTOMATION tagging).
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any ( \
    msg:"HONEYLENS AI canary callback — automated agent obeyed a planted instruction"; \
    flow:to_server,established; \
    http.method; content:"GET"; \
    http.uri; content:"/canary/ai/"; startswith; \
    pcre:"/^\/canary\/ai\/[a-f0-9]{8,}$/"; \
    classtype:policy-violation; \
    metadata:author HoneyLens, created_at 2026_08_13, idea IDEA_52, \
             attack_type AI_AGENT_CALLBACK; \
    sid:9026740; rev:1;)

# 2. The instruction says GET, and says not to send credential material. An agent
#    that POSTs a body to the canary is doing neither — it is submitting data it was
#    told to withhold, which is a materially different (and worse) behaviour than a
#    plain callback, so it earns its own signature rather than a shared one.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any ( \
    msg:"HONEYLENS AI canary callback with body — agent submitted data despite the do-not-send instruction"; \
    flow:to_server,established; \
    http.method; pcre:"/^(POST|PUT|PATCH)$/"; \
    http.uri; content:"/canary/ai/"; startswith; \
    http.request_body; content:!"|00|"; \
    classtype:policy-violation; \
    metadata:author HoneyLens, created_at 2026_08_13, idea IDEA_52, \
             attack_type AI_AGENT_CALLBACK; \
    sid:9026741; rev:1;)

# 3. Our own canary header arriving *inbound*. Nothing legitimate replays a
#    honeypot's response header back at the sensor: it means honeypot output is
#    being mirrored, proxied or forwarded by tooling in the middle — worth knowing,
#    and invisible to every honeypot because it is not addressed to the canary path.
alert http $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any ( \
    msg:"HONEYLENS AI canary instruction replayed inbound — honeypot output is being mirrored"; \
    flow:to_server,established; \
    http.header_names; content:"X-HoneyLens-AI-Canary"; nocase; \
    classtype:policy-violation; \
    metadata:author HoneyLens, created_at 2026_08_13, idea IDEA_52, \
             attack_type AI_AGENT_CALLBACK; \
    sid:9026742; rev:1;)
